\section{Results}
\par 
Figure~\ref{fig:sigfits-SM-inclusive-ratio} presents inclusive four lepton mass distributions for data 
and resulting fitted values of the signal and background pdfs in different final states for the simulations $H+\Z$ fit where 
(gg$\rightarrow$H process is modelled using {\sc powheg+JHUgen}, while qq$\rightarrow$Z process is modelled using {\sc powheg}). 
Distributions are shown in a mass  range $50 < m(4l) < 140$~GeV.
\par
Figure~\ref{fig:inclusive-results_Z4l} shows the inclusive $\Zllll$ fiducial cross section measurements for different final states performed
in the $m_{4\ell}$ range from 50~GeV to 105~GeV, as well as the theoretical prediction obtained using {\sc powheg}. The numerical values of
the independent measurements of the $\Zllll$ fiducial cross sections for are shown in Table~\ref{tab:incresults-simfit}. As the total
uncertainty in the $\Zllll$ inclusive measurement is about 2.6 times lower then the uncertainty in the inclusive $\Hllll$ measurement, 
this result provides also a validation of the measurement procedure in data.  
\par
The $\Zllll$ differential fiducial cross section has also been reported for same set observables studied in $\Hllll$ measurements.
Figure~\ref{fig:differential-results-Z4l} and \ref{fig:differential-results-Z4l-2} shows the measured differential cross section for SM Z$\rightarrow 4\ell$ 
for production and decay related variables. The measured differential cross sections for all observables are  found to be compatible with  
SM-based predictions, within the large associated uncertainties.  The $p$-values for the differential distributions of $\Zllll$ events range from 0.21
in case of rapidity of the Z boson, to 0.99 for some of the angles defined by the four leptons in the Collins-Soper reference frame~\cite{Collins:1977iv}.
\par 
Figures~\ref{fig:inclusive-results-simfit}  and Table~\ref{tab:incresults-simfit} show the results of the  fiducial cross section ratio of  
$\Hllll$ and $\Zllll$ extracted simultaneously. In general, a  good agreement between the measurement results and the theoretical prediction is observed. 
The fitted value for the mass difference between two resonance is found to be $\Delta m = m_H - m_Z = 34.2 \pm 0.7$~GeV, in agreement with the PDG value 
for the Z boson mass and best fit value or $m_{\rm H} = m_{Z}^{PDG} + \Delta m = 125.4 \pm 0.7$~GeV. The Higgs mass result is  found to be compatible
with the dedicated mass measurement results in same final state~\cite{Reference25}. 
% More details on these results can be found 
% in Appendix~\ref{app:H4lZ4lfit}.
